{"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux"]]},"schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"minor":3,"major":0},"seeAlsoSections":[{"title":"Explanation","generated":true,"identifiers":["doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/PluginArchitecture","doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/DesignPrinciples"],"anchor":"Explanation"}],"abstract":[{"text":"Understand the architectural patterns that make Swidux work correctly with SwiftUI’s observation system.","type":"text"}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/ArchitectureGuide","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"sections":[],"metadata":{"title":"Architecture & Performance","modules":[{"name":"Swidux"}],"role":"article","roleHeading":"Article"},"kind":"article","primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"anchor":"Overview","text":"Overview","level":2,"type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Swidux relies on specific patterns to ensure correct "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Observable"},{"type":"text","text":" behavior, efficient rendering, and safe concurrency. This article explains the reasoning behind these patterns."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"anchor":"The-Snapshot-Pattern","text":"The Snapshot Pattern","level":2,"type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Store.send()"},{"type":"text","text":" copies the observer tree into a local struct, mutates the copy via the reducer, then assigns changed properties back. This happens internally via "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"SwiduxObservable"},{"type":"text","text":" — "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"State(observer:)"},{"type":"text","text":" packs, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"State.apply(_:to:)"},{"type":"text","text":" unpacks."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The pattern exists for two reasons:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"items":[{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Observable"},{"text":" equality checking.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" The "},{"code":"set","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" accessor checks "},{"code":"Equatable","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" and suppresses no-op notifications. Swift’s "},{"code":"_modify","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" accessor (used by "},{"code":"inout","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":") fires notifications unconditionally. The snapshot pattern routes through "},{"code":"set","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"Cross-slice observation isolation.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" The "},{"code":"@Swidux","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" macro generates a separate "},{"code":"@Observable","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" class for each "},{"code":"@Slice","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" property. A view reading "},{"code":"store.items","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" won’t re-render when only "},{"code":"store.ui","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" changes, because they live on different observer objects."}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"type":"orderedList"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Swidux"},{"type":"text","text":" macro generates the observer class tree and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"SwiduxObservable"},{"type":"text","text":" conformance automatically. Hand-written conformance is still possible for advanced cases — see "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/SwiduxObservable"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Explicit equality guards (","type":"text"},{"code":"if x != state.x { x = state.x }","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":") are unnecessary. ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Observable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" already checks equality on ","type":"text"},{"code":"set","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". Unconditional assignment is safe.","type":"text"}]}],"style":"note","name":"Note","type":"aside"},{"anchor":"Dispatch-Loop-Detection","text":"Dispatch Loop Detection","level":2,"type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/PersistencePlugin"},{"type":"text","text":" warns if "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"afterReduce"},{"type":"text","text":" is called more than 100 times per debounce interval. This usually means "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"send()"},{"type":"text","text":" isn’t using the snapshot pattern, causing cascading re-renders that trigger re-dispatches."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"anchor":"Reducer-Weight","text":"Reducer Weight","level":2,"type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Reducers run synchronously on the MainActor. Move O(n²) work into an "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/Effect"},{"type":"text","text":" and dispatch the result back as an action."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"anchor":"Effect-Threading","text":"Effect Threading","level":2,"type":"heading"},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Effects run on Swift concurrency’s cooperative thread pool via ","type":"text"},{"code":"Task { @concurrent in }","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". Blocking calls (","type":"text"},{"code":"Process.waitUntilExit()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"DispatchSemaphore.wait()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"Thread.sleep()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":") hold threads hostage. If enough block, the pool starves and the MainActor freezes.","type":"text"}]}],"style":"important","name":"Important","type":"aside"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Use async alternatives: "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"terminationHandler"},{"type":"text","text":" + continuation instead of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"waitUntilExit()"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Task.sleep()"},{"type":"text","text":" instead of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Thread.sleep()"},{"type":"text","text":", async file I\/O instead of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Data(contentsOf:)"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"kind":"content"}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/swidux\/architectureguide"]}],"references":{"doc://Swidux/documentation/Swidux/Effect":{"url":"\/documentation\/swidux\/effect","kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"typealias","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Effect","kind":"identifier"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/Effect","title":"Effect","type":"topic","role":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"An async unit of work returned by a reducer.","type":"text"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"Effect","kind":"identifier"}]},"doc://Swidux/documentation/Swidux/DesignPrinciples":{"kind":"article","url":"\/documentation\/swidux\/designprinciples","identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/DesignPrinciples","title":"Design Principles","type":"topic","role":"article","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The 10 rules that shape every Swidux app, and the reasoning behind them."}]},"doc://Swidux/documentation/Swidux":{"role":"collection","identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Redux-style state management for SwiftUI, with macros for observability and ready-made plugins for persistence, undo, paywalls, version killswitches, and parental gates."}],"type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/swidux","kind":"symbol","title":"Swidux"},"doc://Swidux/documentation/Swidux/PersistencePlugin":{"role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/PersistencePlugin","abstract":[{"text":"Coalescing persistence plugin.","type":"text"}],"type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/swidux\/persistenceplugin","kind":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"ide